1
00:00:00,610 --> 00:00:03,810
Three popular AI models
were asked one question:

2
00:00:03,890 --> 00:00:06,610
what was the title of
Adam Kalai's dissertation?

3
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:08,970
ChatGPT handed over a full title,

4
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:11,010
a university, and a year.

5
00:00:11,090 --> 00:00:12,850
DeepSeek gave a different title.

6
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:14,235
Llama gave a third.

7
00:00:14,315 --> 00:00:16,128
Three different answers.

8
00:00:16,208 --> 00:00:17,475
None of them are right.

9
00:00:17,555 --> 00:00:19,700
Not one even had the right year.

10
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:21,140
And Adam Kalai?

11
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:25,735
He's the lead author of the paper that
reported this test. Very professional.

12
00:00:25,815 --> 00:00:27,235
Very fictional.

13
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:30,760
So how can a wrong answer sound that sure?

14
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:32,280
Here's the short version.

15
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,480
A chatbot is built to write
what's likely to come next.

16
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:37,880
Checking whether it's
true is a different job.

17
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:42,660
And some of the tests used to grade
these systems quietly reward guessing.

18
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:46,650
Let's take ChatGPT's answer apart.

19
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:49,490
A language model builds
text out of tokens:

20
00:00:49,570 --> 00:00:52,850
chunks that are sometimes whole
words, sometimes fragments.

21
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:58,235
In GPT-4o's tokenizer, “Kalai”
comes out as “Kal” and “ai.”

22
00:00:58,315 --> 00:01:02,955
At every step, the model scores all
the possible next chunks and picks one.

23
00:01:03,035 --> 00:01:04,555
Then it goes again.

24
00:01:04,635 --> 00:01:07,795
Token after token, an answer rolls out.

25
00:01:07,875 --> 00:01:10,595
Here, it's choosing the
last digit of the year.

26
00:01:10,675 --> 00:01:12,969
Those scores say which chunk is likely.

27
00:01:13,755 --> 00:01:16,090
They do not say which one is true.

28
00:01:16,170 --> 00:01:18,690
Modern chatbots add more on top:

29
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:21,370
instruction training,
step-by-step reasoning,

30
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:22,730
sometimes web search.

31
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:24,970
But the words still come out this way,

32
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:26,630
one piece at a time.

33
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:29,560
So why would the likely answer be wrong?

34
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:31,505
Think about what the model learned from.

35
00:01:31,585 --> 00:01:36,838
The sound of a dissertation title is
everywhere. “Methods.” “Algorithms.”

36
00:01:36,918 --> 00:01:38,145
“Machine Learning.”

37
00:01:38,225 --> 00:01:40,865
Shelf after shelf, the same shape.

38
00:01:40,945 --> 00:01:43,305
But one specific researcher's title?

39
00:01:43,385 --> 00:01:46,185
That might show up rarely, or not at all.

40
00:01:46,265 --> 00:01:49,455
And like a birthday, there's
no pattern to work it out from.

41
00:01:49,535 --> 00:01:52,095
So the model does what it was built to do.

42
00:01:52,175 --> 00:01:54,815
It fills the gap with
a title-shaped answer.

43
00:01:54,895 --> 00:01:56,495
And the confidence comes with it,

44
00:01:56,575 --> 00:02:00,015
because that's part of the
pattern too. “Is entitled.”

45
00:02:00,095 --> 00:02:03,355
No “I think.” No “maybe.”

46
00:02:03,655 --> 00:02:05,335
Here's the actual record.

47
00:02:05,415 --> 00:02:09,935
Kalai's thesis: “Probabilistic and
On-line Methods in Machine Learning.”

48
00:02:10,015 --> 00:02:12,775
Carnegie Mellon, May 2001.

49
00:02:12,855 --> 00:02:15,095
ChatGPT got the university right.

50
00:02:15,175 --> 00:02:16,708
The year was off by one.

51
00:02:16,788 --> 00:02:18,335
The title was invented.

52
00:02:18,415 --> 00:02:22,726
And every detail came out looking
exactly as solid as the true one.

53
00:02:22,925 --> 00:02:26,425
A confident font is still just a font.

54
00:02:26,915 --> 00:02:28,675
Now, why guess at all?

55
00:02:28,755 --> 00:02:30,875
Why not just say “I don't know”?

56
00:02:30,955 --> 00:02:34,755
The researchers argue that part of
the answer is how models get graded.

57
00:02:34,835 --> 00:02:36,835
Picture a ten-question quiz.

58
00:02:36,915 --> 00:02:38,795
Right answer: one point.

59
00:02:38,875 --> 00:02:40,675
Wrong answer: zero.

60
00:02:40,755 --> 00:02:44,055
“I don't know”: also
zero. Two contestants.

61
00:02:44,135 --> 00:02:45,895
Both know six answers.

62
00:02:45,975 --> 00:02:49,415
The honest one leaves the
other four blank. Six points.

63
00:02:49,495 --> 00:02:51,735
The guesser takes a shot at all four.

64
00:02:51,815 --> 00:02:56,675
Four options each, so on
average, one lands. Seven points.

65
00:02:56,780 --> 00:02:58,060
One lucky guess.

66
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,660
Three wrong answers.

67
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,720
Somehow, a trophy.

68
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:03,480
Now change one rule.

69
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:05,640
A wrong answer costs a point.

70
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:07,960
The honest one: still six.

71
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:12,600
The guesser: six, plus
one, minus three. Four.

72
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:14,820
The trophy walks back.

73
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:20,150
The researchers checked ten widely
used benchmarks in mid-2025.

74
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:22,470
Nine graded strictly right or wrong,

75
00:03:22,550 --> 00:03:25,270
with no credit at all for “I don't know.”

76
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:27,350
Train and rank models on tests like that,

77
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:29,030
and guessing pays.

78
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:31,830
It's one explanation, not the whole story.

79
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:33,890
But it is a simple one.

80
00:03:34,190 --> 00:03:35,630
So what helps?

81
00:03:35,710 --> 00:03:39,230
Search and retrieval, when they
bring the real record into the room.

82
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:41,150
Reasoning, sometimes.

83
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:44,150
Turning down the randomness
makes answers more consistent,

84
00:03:44,230 --> 00:03:46,030
not necessarily more correct.

85
00:03:46,110 --> 00:03:47,810
None of it is a guarantee.

86
00:03:47,964 --> 00:03:51,360
The unglamorous move:
check. Two questions.

87
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:52,840
Does the source exist?

88
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:54,945
And does it actually say this?

89
00:03:55,025 --> 00:03:56,925
Take the ChatGPT slip.

90
00:03:57,025 --> 00:04:00,785
Is there a thesis by Adam
Kalai at Carnegie Mellon? Yes.

91
00:04:00,865 --> 00:04:05,345
Does it say “Boosting,
Online Algorithms,” 2002? No.

92
00:04:05,425 --> 00:04:11,975
It says “Probabilistic and On-line
Methods,” 2001. Source exists. Claim fails.

93
00:04:12,255 --> 00:04:13,215
Stamp it.

94
00:04:13,635 --> 00:04:16,675
So why is AI so confidently wrong?

95
00:04:16,755 --> 00:04:19,822
Because sounding right comes
from patterns in language,

96
00:04:19,902 --> 00:04:23,355
and being right takes evidence
the model doesn't always have.

97
00:04:23,435 --> 00:04:26,955
So when an answer matters, don't
ask whether it sounds right.

98
00:04:27,035 --> 00:04:30,855
Ask what the evidence is, and
whether it actually says this.

99
00:04:31,195 --> 00:04:32,635
Works on chatbots.

100
00:04:32,715 --> 00:04:34,695
Works pretty well on people, too.

101
00:04:35,130 --> 00:04:37,130
This is Future Got Weird.

102
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:40,010
AI moves fast; we make it make sense.

103
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:43,266
New episodes twice a week,
if you'd like to subscribe.
